  "paper": "paper_2",
  "question": {
    "html": "<section class=\"context\"><p>One of Kepler’s laws suggests that for moons that have circular orbits around a planet:</p>\n<p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{{T}^{2}}{4 π {r}^{3}} = k\"></span></p>\n<p>where <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T\"></span> is the orbital period of the moon, <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"r\"></span> is the radius of its circular orbit about the planet, and <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"k\"></span> is a constant.</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"a\"><p><strong>a</strong> Show that <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"k=\\frac{1}{GM}\"></span>. [2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"b\"><p><strong>b</strong> </p><p>The table gives data relating to the two moons of Mars.</p>\n<table><tbody><tr><td><p>Moon</p></td><td><p>T / hour</p></td><td><p>r / Mm</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Phobos</p></td><td><p>7.66</p></td><td><p>9.38</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Deimos</p></td><td><p>30.4</p></td><td><p>-</p></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Determine r for Deimos.</p><p>[2]</p></section><section class=\"question-part\" data-label=\"c\"><p><strong>c</strong> Determine the mass of Mars. [3]</p></section>"
